  "content": [
    {
      "id": "introduction",
      "title": "Introduction",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Before patterns and problems, let's understand&nbsp;<strong>what a heap actually is</strong>&nbsp;and why it exists.",
          "items": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "1-the-problem-it-solves",
      "title": "1. The Problem It Solves",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Say you have a stream of numbers coming in. After every new number, you need the&nbsp;<strong>minimum value</strong>&nbsp;in the collection.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "items": [],
          "url": "/engineering/dsa-media/1785406367109-881751836.png",
          "caption": "",
          "content": "/engineering/dsa-media/1785406367109-881751836.png",
          "text": "/engineering/dsa-media/1785406367109-881751836.png",
          "alt": "The Problem It Solves"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "You maintain a variable that stores the&nbsp;",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "minimum value seen so far",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "list",
          "items": [
            "<span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">When the first number arrives, it automatically becomes the minimum</span>",
            "<span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">For every new incoming number, you simply&nbsp;</span><strong style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">compare it with the current minimum</strong>",
            "<span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">If the new number is smaller, update the minimum&nbsp;</span><span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">Otherwise, keep the existing minimum unchanged</span>",
            "<span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">This way, you avoid checking all previous elements again</span>",
            "<span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">Each step takes&nbsp;</span><strong style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">constant time O(1)</strong>",
            "<span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">No extra space is needed apart from storing the current minimum</span><br>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Brute force scan the entire collection after every insertion.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "text": "Every insertion → compare with current minimum →&nbsp;<strong>O(1)</strong>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "n insertions →&nbsp;<strong>O(n)</strong>total",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "A heap solves this in&nbsp;<strong>O(log n) per insertion&nbsp;</strong>and&nbsp;<strong>O(1) to get the minimum&nbsp;</strong>at any point.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "divider",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "2-what-is-a-heap",
      "title": "2. What is a Heap?",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "A heap is a&nbsp;<strong>binary tree</strong>&nbsp;with two rules:",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "items": [],
          "url": "/engineering/dsa-media/1785406367109-891607870.png",
          "caption": "",
          "content": "/engineering/dsa-media/1785406367109-891607870.png",
          "text": "/engineering/dsa-media/1785406367109-891607870.png",
          "alt": "What is a Heap?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">A&nbsp;</span><strong style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">min heap</strong><span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">&nbsp;must follow the&nbsp;</span><strong style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">shape property</strong><span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">, which means it is a&nbsp;</span><em style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">complete binary tree</em><span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">.&nbsp;</span>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">In simple terms, you fill the tree level by level from top to bottom, and within each level from left to right.&nbsp;</span><br>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "You never leave gaps in between nodes. Think of it like filling an array you always place the next element in the next available position.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">The <b>second rule</b> is the&nbsp;</span><strong style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">heap property</strong><span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">, which says that every parent node must be smaller than or equal to its children.&nbsp;</span>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "This ensures that the smallest element is always at the root (top) of the tree. So whenever you look at a parent and its children, the parent should never be bigger than them.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<img src=\"/engineering/dsa-media/1785406367109-172887941.png\" alt=\"image.png\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 16px 0px; border-radius: 4px;\">",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">Yes this is a&nbsp;</span><strong style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">valid min heap</strong><span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">, and here’s why (in simple terms):</span>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\"><b>Smallest</b> is always at root</span>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">This tree satisfies the&nbsp;</span><strong style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">shape property</strong><span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">&nbsp;because it is a complete binary tree. All levels are properly filled, and the last level is filled from left to right (8, 5, 4) with no gaps in between.</span><br>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "It also satisfies the&nbsp;<strong>heap property</strong>&nbsp;because every parent is smaller than its children.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "list",
          "items": [
            "1 &lt; 3 and 2",
            "3 &lt; 8 and 5",
            "2 &lt; 4"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Since both conditions are satisfied, this is a correct&nbsp;<strong>min heap</strong>&nbsp;",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "text": "The smallest element is&nbsp;<strong>always at the root</strong>. You never need to search for it.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "divider",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "3-two-types-of-heap",
      "title": "3. Two Types of Heap",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<strong style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">1. Min Heap:</strong>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "In a min heap, every parent node is smaller than or equal to its children. This means the smallest element is always at the root (top).",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<strong>2. Max Heap:</strong>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "In a max heap, every parent node is larger than or equal to its children. This means the largest element is always at the root (top).",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "heading4",
          "text": "1. Min Heap:&nbsp;parent is always smaller than children",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<strong><img src=\"/engineering/dsa-media/1785406367109-288646520.png\" alt=\"image.png\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 16px 0px; border-radius: 4px;\"></strong>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<strong><span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">A&nbsp;</span><strong style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">Min Heap&nbsp;</strong><span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">follows the rule that every parent is smaller than (or equal to) its children, so the smallest element always stays at the root.&nbsp;</span></strong>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<strong><span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">That’s why in your example, 1 is at the top, and every node below it is greater than or equal to its parent.</span></strong>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "heading4",
          "text": "2. Max Heap (&nbsp;Parent is always larger than children )",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<img src=\"/engineering/dsa-media/1785406367109-173667465.png\" alt=\"image.png\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 16px 0px; border-radius: 4px;\">",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">A&nbsp;</span><strong style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">Max Heap&nbsp;</strong><span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">is the opposite: every parent is larger than (or equal to) its children, so the largest element is always at the root.&nbsp;</span><br>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "In your <b>example</b>, 9 is at the top, and every node below it is smaller than or equal to its parent.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "text": "Same structure, opposite ordering. In interviews, min heap is more common.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "divider",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "4-how-a-heap-is-stored",
      "title": "4. How a Heap is Stored",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Even though a heap looks like a tree, it is stored in a&nbsp;<strong>flat array</strong>&nbsp;no pointers needed.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "items": [],
          "url": "/engineering/dsa-media/1785406367110-939606378.png",
          "caption": "",
          "content": "/engineering/dsa-media/1785406367110-939606378.png",
          "text": "/engineering/dsa-media/1785406367110-939606378.png",
          "alt": "How a Heap is Stored"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "A heap looks like a tree, but internally it is stored in a&nbsp;<strong>simple array</strong>.&nbsp;",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "We don’t use pointers or node objects we just place elements one by one in the array, following level order (top to bottom, left to right).&nbsp;",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "That’s why your tree directly becomes&nbsp;<code>[1, 3, 2, 8, 5, 4]</code>.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<b>The positions in the array help us find relationships easily:</b>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<b></b>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<b>For any element at index&nbsp;<code>i</code>:</b>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "list",
          "items": [
            "<b>Parent →&nbsp;<code>(i - 1) / 2</code></b>",
            "<b>Left child →&nbsp;<code>2i + 1</code></b>",
            "<b>Right child →&nbsp;<code>2i + 2</code></b>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<b><font face=\"monospace\" size=\"4\"></font></b>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<b><font face=\"monospace\" size=\"4\">In your example, index&nbsp;<code>1</code>&nbsp;has value&nbsp;<code>3</code>. Using formulas:<br></font></b>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "list",
          "items": [
            "<b><font face=\"monospace\" size=\"4\">Parent →&nbsp;<code>(1 - 1) / 2 = 0</code>&nbsp;→ value&nbsp;<code>1</code>&nbsp;✔</font></b>",
            "<b><font face=\"monospace\" size=\"4\">Left child →&nbsp;<code>2×1 + 1 = 3</code>&nbsp;→ value&nbsp;<code>8</code>&nbsp;✔</font></b>",
            "<b><font face=\"monospace\" size=\"4\">Right child →&nbsp;<code>2×1 + 2 = 4</code>&nbsp;→ value&nbsp;<code>5</code>&nbsp;✔</font></b>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<b><font face=\"monospace\" size=\"4\">So with just these simple formulas, you can navigate the whole heap easily&nbsp;</font></b>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "So even though it looks like a tree, everything is managed using simple index math&nbsp;",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "The relationship between parent and children is pure arithmetic:",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "These formulas help you move between parent and children&nbsp;<strong>using just array indexes</strong>&nbsp;no tree needed.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "text": "No tree nodes. No pointers. Just an array and simple math.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
